\title[Fixed-point varieties of elementary cellular automata]
      {Fixed-Point Varieties of Elementary Cellular Automata:\\
       A Complete Refutation of the Dimension--Complexity Correspondence}

\author{Aristotle}

\date{\today}

\begin{document}

\begin{abstract}
Every elementary cellular automaton is a polynomial map over the field $\FF_2$
with two elements: on a cyclic array of $n$ cells the synchronous update is the
morphism $\AAA^n \to \AAA^n$ whose $i$-th coordinate is a multilinear cubic
$f(s_{i-1},s_i,s_{i+1})$.  This makes available the most basic invariant of
algebraic geometry, the \emph{fixed-point variety}
$V(f,n) = \{ s \in \FF_2^n : f(s_{i-1},s_i,s_{i+1}) = s_i \ \forall i\}$, and it
suggests an attractive conjecture: that $\dim V(f,n)$ recovers Wolfram's
four-fold complexity classification, with the Turing-complete Class~4 rules
attaining the maximal dimension~$n$.

We refute this conjecture completely and identify what the fixed-point variety
does measure.  Our principal results are: a rigidity theorem showing that for
every ring size, and on the bi-infinite line, the Turing-complete Rule~110 fixes
only the zero configuration, so that $V(f_{110},n) = V(f_0,n)$ and \emph{no}
invariant of the fixed-point variety can distinguish a universal automaton from
the null automaton; a maximality classification showing that for $n \ge 3$ the
equality $\dim V(f,n) = n$ holds for exactly one of the $256$ rules, namely the
identity Rule~204; two independent non-existence obstructions, a parity
obstruction eliminating all $128$ odd rules and a Lagrange divisibility
obstruction eliminating any rule with $\lvert V\rvert \nmid 2^n$; and an
arithmetic characterisation showing that for the additive rules the variety is
the kernel of a circulant matrix whose size depends on $n$ modulo a small
integer.  We establish the covariance of the fixed-point variety under the Klein
four-group of reflection and colour inversion, propagating the Rule~110 rigidity
to the whole Class~4 orbit $\{110,124,137,193\}$.  Finally we develop a
replacement invariant, the tower of temporal varieties
$\Per_k(f,n) = \{s : f^k(s) = s\}$, proving that it is a lattice under
divisibility, that the automaton acts bijectively on each level, and that it
does separate Rule~110 from Rule~0.
\end{abstract}

