\section{Introduction}

\subsection{Two languages for one object}

An elementary cellular automaton (ECA) is specified by a local map
$f : \{0,1\}^3 \to \{0,1\}$; there are $2^8 = 256$ of them, indexed by Wolfram's
convention in which the binary digits of the rule number list the outputs on the
eight neighbourhoods $(l,c,r)$ read as $4l+2c+r$.  Despite the triviality of the
specification, the family contains a universal computer (Rule~110), a rule whose
output columns pass most statistical tests for randomness (Rule~30), and rules
reproducing the Sierpi\'nski gasket (Rules~90 and~150).  Wolfram's empirical
classification sorts the $256$ rules into four classes: uniform~(1),
periodic~(2), chaotic~(3), and complex/computational~(4).

The classification is qualitative and there is no known algorithm producing it.
It is therefore natural to look for a computable algebraic invariant which
reproduces it, and one candidate presents itself immediately.

Over $\FF_2$, where $x^2 = x$, every function $\FF_2^3 \to \FF_2$ has a unique
representation as a multilinear polynomial
\[
  f(l,c,r) = a_\emptyset + a_l\,l + a_c\,c + a_r\,r
           + a_{lc}\,lc + a_{lr}\,lr + a_{cr}\,cr + a_{lcr}\,lcr,
\]
the \emph{algebraic normal form}, obtained from the truth table by the
Reed--M\"uller (M\"obius) transform $a_S = \sum_{T \subseteq S} f(T)$.  The eight
$\FF_2$-coefficients account for all $256$ rules; $128$ have degree~$3$, $112$
degree~$2$, $14$ degree~$1$ and $2$ degree~$0$.  Some standard examples:

\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{@{}rll@{}}
\toprule
Rule & Algebraic normal form & Degree \\
\midrule
$0$   & $0$                    & $0$ \\
$204$ & $c$                    & $1$ \\
$90$  & $l + r$                & $1$ \\
$150$ & $l + c + r$            & $1$ \\
$30$  & $l + c + r + cr$       & $2$ \\
$45$  & $1 + l + r + cr$       & $2$ \\
$232$ & $lc + lr + cr$         & $2$ \\
$110$ & $c + r + cr + lcr$     & $3$ \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{center}

Thus an automaton on a cyclic array of $n$ cells is a morphism of affine
$n$-space over $\FF_2$ to itself, of degree at most three.

\subsection{The conjecture under test}

\begin{conjecture}[The Dimension Conjecture]\label{conj:dim}
Let $f$ be an elementary rule and $V(f,n)$ its fixed-point variety on the ring of
$n$ cells.  Then $\dim V(f,n) = 0$ for Wolfram Class~1, $\dim V(f,n) \le n/2$ for
Class~2, $\dim V(f,n) \ge n/2$ for Class~3, and $\dim V(f,n) = n$ for Class~4.
\end{conjecture}

The conjecture has surface plausibility: Rule~0 (Class~1) has $V = \{0\}$ of
dimension~$0$, and Rule~204 has $V = \AAA^n$ of dimension~$n$.  One might further
hope that a sheaf-theoretic refinement, regarding each automaton as defining a
sheaf on configuration space whose global sections classify stable
configurations, would yield a finer invariant, with Rule~110's sheaf having the
richest section structure.  Every clause is false, and the sheaf-theoretic
refinement fails \emph{a fortiori}: by Theorem~\ref{thm:blind} the underlying
varieties of Rules~110 and~0 coincide as sets.

\section{The fixed-point variety}

Throughout, $\FF_2 = \ZZ/2$ and $n \ge 0$.  Cells are indexed by $\ZZ/n$; the
degenerate case $n=0$ is read as $\ZZ$, so all statements quantified over $n$
include the bi-infinite line.

\begin{definition}[Configuration space]
The \emph{configuration space of size $n$} is
$\Cfg(n) = \{ s : \ZZ/n \to \FF_2\}$, the set of $\FF_2$-points of affine
$n$-space $\AAA^n_{\FF_2}$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Local rule and update]
For $0 \le \rho < 256$ let $f_\rho : \FF_2^3 \to \FF_2$ take at $(l,c,r)$ the
value of bit $4l+2c+r$ of $\rho$.  The \emph{global update} is
$F_\rho : \Cfg(n) \to \Cfg(n)$, $(F_\rho s)_i = f_\rho(s_{i-1},s_i,s_{i+1})$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Fixed-point variety]
$V(\rho,n) = \{ s \in \Cfg(n) : F_\rho(s) = s\}$; equivalently $s \in V(\rho,n)$
iff $f_\rho(s_{i-1},s_i,s_{i+1}) = s_i$ for every $i$.  This is the
$\FF_2$-point set of the affine subscheme of $\AAA^n$ cut out by the $n$ cubics
$f_\rho(x_{i-1},x_i,x_{i+1}) - x_i$.
\end{definition}

\begin{proposition}[Shift invariance]\label{prop:shift}
If $s \in V(\rho,n)$ and $k \in \ZZ/n$ then the rotate $i \mapsto s_{i+k}$ lies in
$V(\rho,n)$.
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}
The defining condition at position $i$ for the rotate is the defining condition
at position $i+k$ for $s$.
\end{proof}

Thus $V(\rho,n)$ is a \emph{cyclic subshift of finite type}: a set of cyclic
words determined by a list of admissible three-letter windows.  This is the
structural fact behind Conjecture~\ref{conj:trace}.

\begin{definition}[Additive rule]
A rule $\rho$ is \emph{additive} if $f_\rho$ is $\FF_2$-linear in its three
arguments; equivalently its algebraic normal form has no constant term and no
monomial of degree $\ge 2$.  There are exactly eight additive rules:
$0,60,90,102,150,170,204,240$.
\end{definition}

\begin{proposition}\label{prop:additive}
If $\rho$ is additive then $f_\rho(0,0,0) = 0$ and $V(\rho,n)$ is an
$\FF_2$-linear subspace of $\Cfg(n)$.
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}
Setting all six arguments to $0$ in the additivity relation gives
$f_\rho(0,0,0) = 2f_\rho(0,0,0) = 0$, so $0 \in V(\rho,n)$.  If $a,b \in V(\rho,n)$
then at each $i$ additivity gives
$f_\rho(a_{i-1}+b_{i-1}, a_i+b_i, a_{i+1}+b_{i+1}) = a_i + b_i$, so
$a+b \in V(\rho,n)$; closure under the scalars $0,1$ is trivial.
\end{proof}

\begin{definition}[Dimension]\label{def:dim}
We say $V(\rho,n)$ \emph{has dimension} $d$ if there is an $\FF_2$-subspace
$W \subseteq \Cfg(n)$ with $W = V(\rho,n)$ as sets and $\dim_{\FF_2} W = d$.
\end{definition}

\begin{proposition}[Well-definedness and cardinality]\label{prop:card}
The dimension, when it exists, is unique; and if $\dim V(\rho,n) = d$ with
$n \ge 1$ then $\lvert V(\rho,n)\rvert = 2^d$.
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}
Two subspaces with the same underlying set are equal, so their dimensions agree;
a $d$-dimensional $\FF_2$-space has $2^d$ elements.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Seed rigidity caps the dimension at two]\label{thm:seed}
Let $n \ge 1$ and suppose every $s \in V(\rho,n)$ with $s_0 = s_1 = 0$ is
identically zero.  Then $\dim V(\rho,n) \le 2$ whenever the dimension exists,
however large $n$ is.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
The evaluation $\pi : \Cfg(n) \to \FF_2^2$, $s \mapsto (s_0,s_1)$, is
$\FF_2$-linear; its restriction to $W = V(\rho,n)$ has trivial kernel by
hypothesis, so $\pi|_W$ is injective and $\dim W \le 2$.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[Period transfer]\label{lem:transfer}
Let $n \ge 1$ and $s \in \Cfg(n)$ satisfy $s_{i+p} = s_i$ for all $i$, where
$\gcd(p,n)=1$.  Then $s$ is constant.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
Iterating gives $s_{i+pk} = s_i$ for every $k \ge 0$.  Since $p$ is invertible
modulo $n$, choose $k$ with $pk \equiv 1 \pmod n$; then $s_{i+1} = s_i$ for all
$i$, and induction along $i = 0,1,2,\dots$ gives $s_i = s_0$.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[Period-two seeds]\label{lem:seedtwo}
Let $n \ge 1$ and let $s,t \in \Cfg(n)$ both satisfy $x_{i+2} = x_i$.  If
$s_0 = t_0$ and $s_1 = t_1$ then $s = t$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
A simultaneous induction on $k$ shows $s_k = t_k$ and $s_{k+1} = t_{k+1}$ for all
natural $k$; every residue is of the form $k \bmod n$.
\end{proof}

\section{Rigidity of Rule 110}

Rule~110 has algebraic normal form $f_{110} = c + r + cr + lcr$, so stationarity
$f_{110}(l,c,r) = c$ becomes
\begin{equation}\label{eq:110}
  r + cr + lcr = 0, \qquad\text{i.e.}\qquad r\,(1 + c + lc) = 0.
\end{equation}

\begin{lemma}[Local rigidity]\label{lem:110loc}
For $l,c,r \in \FF_2$, $f_{110}(l,c,r) = c$ if and only if $r = 0$, or ($c=1$ and
$l=0$).
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
By \eqref{eq:110} the condition holds iff $r = 0$ or $1 + c(1+l) = 0$, and
$c(1+l) = 1$ iff $c = 1$ and $l = 0$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Rigidity of Rule 110]\label{thm:110}
For every $n \ge 0$ --- including $n=0$, i.e.\ the bi-infinite configuration
space $\ZZ \to \FF_2$ --- we have $V(110,n) = \{0\}$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
The zero configuration is stationary since $f_{110}(0,0,0) = 0$.  Conversely let
$s \in V(110,n)$ and suppose $s_i = 1$ for some $i$.  Apply
Lemma~\ref{lem:110loc} to the window $(s_{i-2},s_{i-1},s_i)$ centred at $i-1$:
its right entry is $s_i = 1 \ne 0$, so
\begin{equation}\label{eq:110step}
  s_{i-1} = 1 \quad\text{and}\quad s_{i-2} = 0 .
\end{equation}
Apply Lemma~\ref{lem:110loc} to the window $(s_{i-3},s_{i-2},s_{i-1})$ centred at
$i-2$: its right entry is $s_{i-1} = 1 \ne 0$ by \eqref{eq:110step}, so the second
alternative again holds, giving $s_{i-2} = 1$ and contradicting
\eqref{eq:110step}.  Hence no cell carries a $1$.
\end{proof}

The argument uses two applications of a purely local constraint; it is uniform in
$n$, needs no induction on the ring size, and applies verbatim on $\ZZ$.

\begin{corollary}\label{cor:110}
Rule~110 fixes exactly one configuration on every ring; $\dim V(110,n) = 0$; and
$2\dim V(110,n) < n$ for every $n \ge 1$.  Rule~110 therefore fails not only the
Class~4 prediction $\dim = n$ but even the weaker Class~3 prediction
$\dim \ge n/2$.
\end{corollary}

\begin{theorem}[The fixed-point variety cannot detect universality]\label{thm:blind}
For every $n$, $V(110,n) = V(0,n)$.  Consequently, for every predicate $P$ on
subsets of $\Cfg(n)$,
\[
  P\bigl(V(110,n)\bigr) \iff P\bigl(V(0,n)\bigr).
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
$f_0 \equiv 0$, so $F_0(s) = 0$ for every $s$ and $F_0(s) = s$ forces $s = 0$;
thus $V(0,n) = \{0\}$.  Combine with Theorem~\ref{thm:110}.
\end{proof}

This is the decisive obstruction: not merely does dimension fail to separate the
Turing-complete rule from the null rule, but no invariant whatsoever of the
fixed-point variety can separate them, since the two varieties are equal as sets
--- hence carry the same sheaves and the same cohomology.

\section{Maximal dimension classifies the identity automaton}

\begin{lemma}[Independence of local windows]\label{lem:window}
Let $n \ge 3$.  For any $(l,c,r) \in \FF_2^3$ there is $s \in \Cfg(n)$ with
$s_0 = l$, $s_1 = c$, $s_2 = r$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
On a ring of size at least $3$ the residues $0,1,2$ are pairwise distinct, so the
assignment is consistent; extend by $0$.
\end{proof}

\begin{proposition}\label{prop:univ}
Let $n \ge 3$.  Then $V(\rho,n) = \AAA^n$ if and only if $f_\rho(l,c,r) = c$ for
all $(l,c,r)$.
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}
($\Leftarrow$) Immediate.  ($\Rightarrow$) Given $(l,c,r)$ choose $s$ as in
Lemma~\ref{lem:window}; stationarity at cell $1$ reads $f_\rho(l,c,r) = c$.
\end{proof}

\begin{proposition}\label{prop:204}
For $\rho < 256$, $f_\rho$ is the centre projection $(l,c,r)\mapsto c$ if and only
if $\rho = 204$.
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}
The centre projection outputs $1$ exactly on the neighbourhoods with $c = 1$,
i.e.\ on the indices $2,3,6,7$, so its Wolfram number is
$4+8+64+128 = 204$; conversely $204 = 11001100_2$ has precisely these bits set,
and a rule below $256$ is determined by its eight low bits.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Maximality classification]\label{thm:max}
Let $n \ge 3$ and $\rho < 256$.  Then $\dim V(\rho,n) = n$ if and only if
$\rho = 204$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
If $\dim V(\rho,n) = n$ then the witnessing subspace has full dimension
$n = \dim \Cfg(n)$, so it is all of $\Cfg(n)$ and $V(\rho,n) = \AAA^n$; apply
Propositions~\ref{prop:univ} and~\ref{prop:204}.  Conversely for $\rho = 204$ we
have $V = \AAA^n$, a subspace of dimension $n$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}
None of the Class~4 rules $110,124,137,193$ has $\dim V = n$ for any $n \ge 3$.
The unique rule satisfying the conjecture's Class~4 prediction is the identity
automaton, which has no dynamics at all.
\end{corollary}

\section{Two obstructions to the existence of a dimension}

\subsection{The parity obstruction}

\begin{lemma}\label{lem:parity}
$f_\rho(0,0,0) = 1$ if and only if $\rho$ is odd.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
$f_\rho(0,0,0)$ is bit $0$ of $\rho$, i.e.\ $\rho \bmod 2$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Half the family has no dimension]\label{thm:odd}
If $\rho$ is odd then $0 \notin V(\rho,n)$ for every $n$, so $V(\rho,n)$ is not a
linear subspace and $\dim V(\rho,n)$ does not exist.  This eliminates $128$ of
the $256$ rules, for every ring size.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
By Lemma~\ref{lem:parity} the constraint at any cell of the zero configuration
reads $1 = 0$; a linear subspace always contains $0$.
\end{proof}

Rules $45$, $137$ and $193$ --- one Class~3 rule and two Class~4 rules --- are
among the eliminated.

\subsection{The Lagrange obstruction}

\begin{definition}
A set $S \subseteq \Cfg(n)$ is an \emph{affine subvariety} if $S = v + W$ for some
$v \in \Cfg(n)$ and some $\FF_2$-subspace $W$.  This is the weakest reasonable
reading of ``$S$ has a dimension''.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Lagrange obstruction]\label{thm:lagrange}
If $S \subseteq \Cfg(n)$ is a non-empty affine subvariety with $n \ge 1$ then
$\lvert S\rvert$ divides $2^n$; in particular it is a power of two.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Translation by $v$ is a bijection, so $\lvert S\rvert = \lvert W\rvert$; and $W$
is a subgroup of the additive group $\Cfg(n) \cong (\ZZ/2)^n$ of order $2^n$, so
Lagrange's theorem applies.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}\label{cor:lagrange}
If $\lvert V(\rho,n)\rvert \nmid 2^n$ then $V(\rho,n)$ is not an affine
subvariety and $\dim V(\rho,n)$ does not exist in any sense.
\end{corollary}

\begin{proposition}[The majority rule]
Rule~232, $f = lc+lr+cr$, satisfies $f_{232}(l,c,r) = c$ iff $l = c$ or $r = c$.
On the ring of size $4$ it has exactly $6$ stationary configurations --- the two
constants and four domain-wall patterns --- and $6 \nmid 16$.  Hence $V(232,4)$
is not an affine subvariety.
\end{proposition}

\begin{proposition}
On the ring of size $3$, Rule~45 has exactly $3$ stationary configurations, the
three rotations of the pulse train $100$, and $3 \nmid 8$.  Hence $V(45,3)$ is
not an affine subvariety.
\end{proposition}

Exhaustive computation on the ring of size $6$ shows that only $91$ of the $256$
rules have a fixed-point locus that is a linear subspace; for the remaining $165$
the conjecture's central quantity is undefined.

\begin{theorem}[The Dimension Conjecture is false in four independent ways]
\label{thm:false}
\begin{enumerate}
\item The Class~4 rule $110$ has $V(110,8) = V(0,8)$ and $\dim V(110,8) \ne 8$.
\item The Class~3 rule $90$ satisfies $2\dim V(90,n) < n$ for every $n \ge 5$.
\item The Class~3 rule $45$ has $V(45,8) = \varnothing$ while
      $V(45,9) \ne \varnothing$.
\item The Class~2 rule $232$ has $\lvert V(232,4)\rvert = 6$ and no dimension at
      all.
\end{enumerate}
Each item is independently fatal.
\end{theorem}

\section{What the variety really measures: arithmetic}

\subsection{Rule 30: a three-point locus}

Rule~30 has $f_{30} = l+c+r+cr$, so stationarity reads
\begin{equation}\label{eq:30}
  l + r + cr = 0,
  \qquad\text{i.e.}\qquad
  \bigl(s_i = 0 \Rightarrow s_{i-1} = s_{i+1}\bigr)
  \ \text{ and }\
  \bigl(s_i = 1 \Rightarrow s_{i-1} = 0\bigr).
\end{equation}

\begin{lemma}[Transfer relation]\label{lem:30}
If $s \in V(30,n)$ then $s_{i+2} = s_i$ for all $i$: every stationary
configuration of Rule~30 has spatial period two.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
A finite check on $\FF_2^4$ shows that $f_{30}(a,b,c) = b$ and $f_{30}(b,c,d) = c$
imply $c = a$; apply with $(a,b,c,d) = (s_i,s_{i+1},s_{i+2},s_{i+3})$ using the
constraints at cells $i+1$ and $i+2$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Rule 30 on odd rings]
If $n$ is odd then $V(30,n) = \{0\}$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
By Lemma~\ref{lem:30} and Lemma~\ref{lem:transfer} (with $p=2$) every stationary
configuration is constant; a constant $x$ satisfies \eqref{eq:30} iff
$x + x + x\cdot x = 0$, i.e.\ $x = 0$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Rule 30 on even rings]\label{thm:30even}
Let $n \ge 2$ be even, let $\alpha_i = i \bmod 2$ be the alternating
configuration (well defined since $2 \mid n$) and $\bar\alpha = 1 + \alpha$ its
colour complement.  Then $V(30,n) = \{0, \alpha, \bar\alpha\}$, a set of exactly
three distinct configurations.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
All three are stationary: for $\alpha$, the neighbours $i-1$ and $i+1$ have equal
parity so $l + r = 0$, while $c$ and $r$ have opposite parity so $cr = 0$, and
\eqref{eq:30} holds; the same computation applies to $\bar\alpha$, and $0$ is
immediate.  Conversely a stationary $s$ has period two by Lemma~\ref{lem:30},
hence by Lemma~\ref{lem:seedtwo} is determined by $(s_0,s_1)$, giving four
candidates $0, \alpha, \bar\alpha, 1$; the all-ones configuration fails since
$f_{30}(1,1,1) = 0 \ne 1$.  Distinctness is checked at cells $0$ and $1$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[No dimension, for infinitely many $n$]
For every even $n \ge 2$, $\lvert V(30,n)\rvert = 3 \nmid 2^n$; hence $V(30,n)$
is not an affine subvariety and $\dim V(30,n)$ does not exist.
\end{corollary}

\subsection{Rule 90: the prime 3}

Rule~90 is additive, $f_{90} = l+r$; stationarity reads
$s_{i-1}+s_i+s_{i+1} = 0$, i.e.\ the recurrence $s_{i+1} = s_{i-1}+s_i$ with
characteristic polynomial $x^2+x+1$, whose roots are the primitive cube roots of
unity in $\FF_4$.

\begin{lemma}\label{lem:90}
If $s \in V(90,n)$ then $s_{i+3} = s_i$ for all $i$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
A finite check shows $f_{90}(a,b,c) = b$ and $f_{90}(b,c,d) = c$ imply $d = a$;
apply at cells $i+1$ and $i+2$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Mod-3 dichotomy for Rule 90]\label{thm:90}
\begin{enumerate}
\item If $3 \nmid n$ then $V(90,n) = \{0\}$, so $\dim V(90,n) = 0$.
\item If $3 \mid n$ and $n \ne 0$ then $V(90,n)$ contains the period-three wave
      $w$ with $w_i = 0$ iff $3 \mid i$, so it is strictly larger.
\item For every $n \ge 1$, $\dim V(90,n) \le 2$.
\end{enumerate}
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
(1) By Lemma~\ref{lem:90} and Lemma~\ref{lem:transfer} with $p=3$ a stationary
configuration is constant, say $\equiv x$; stationarity reads $x + x = x$, so
$x = 0$.  (2) Direct verification through the ring homomorphism
$\ZZ/n \to \ZZ/3$, available when $3 \mid n$, reduces the claim to three cases.
(3) The recurrence shows a stationary configuration vanishing at cells $0$ and
$1$ vanishes identically; apply Theorem~\ref{thm:seed}.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}
For $n \ge 5$, $2\dim V(90,n) < n$: the Class~3 rule $90$ violates the predicted
$\dim \ge n/2$ for every large ring.
\end{corollary}

\subsection{Rule 45: existence itself is arithmetic}

\begin{lemma}\label{lem:45}
If $s \in V(45,n)$ then $s_{i+3} = s_i$ for all $i$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
A finite check shows: if $f_{45}(a,b,c) = b$, $f_{45}(b,c,d) = c$ and
$f_{45}(c,d,e) = d$, then $d = a$; apply at cells $i+1,i+2,i+3$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Existence criterion for Rule 45]\label{thm:45}
$V(45,n) \ne \varnothing$ if and only if $3 \mid n$.  When $3 \mid n$ the pulse
train $p$ with $p_i = 1$ iff $3 \mid i$ is stationary.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
If $3 \nmid n$ then by Lemmas~\ref{lem:45} and~\ref{lem:transfer} a stationary
configuration is constant $\equiv x$; but
$f_{45}(x,x,x) = 1 + x + x + x\cdot x = 1 + x \ne x$, a contradiction.  If
$3 \mid n$ the reduction $\ZZ/n \to \ZZ/3$ transports the three-case verification
for $p$.
\end{proof}

\subsection{Rule 150: the prime 2}

Rule~150 is additive, $f_{150} = l+c+r$; stationarity reads $l + r = 0$, i.e.\
$s_{i-1} = s_{i+1}$, so the variety is the space of period-two configurations.

\begin{theorem}\label{thm:150}
\begin{enumerate}
\item Every $s \in V(150,n)$ satisfies $s_{i+2} = s_i$.
\item If $n$ is odd then $V(150,n) = \{0,1\}$, and $\dim V(150,n) = 1$.
\item If $n$ is even then the alternating configuration $\alpha_i = i \bmod 2$ is
      a non-constant element of $V(150,n)$, whose dimension is then $2$.
\item For every $n \ge 1$, $\dim V(150,n) \le 2$; hence $2\dim V(150,n) < n$ for
      $n \ge 5$.
\end{enumerate}
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
(1) is the stationarity relation at cell $i+1$.  (2) By (1) and
Lemma~\ref{lem:transfer} a stationary configuration on an odd ring is constant,
and both constants satisfy $f_{150}(x,x,x) = 3x = x$; Proposition~\ref{prop:card}
gives dimension $1$.  (3) Reduce $\ZZ/n \to \ZZ/2$; then
$\alpha_{i-1} = \alpha_{i+1}$ and $\alpha_0 \ne \alpha_1$.  (4) A stationary
configuration vanishing at $0,1$ vanishes identically by (1) and
Lemma~\ref{lem:seedtwo}; apply Theorem~\ref{thm:seed}.
\end{proof}

\subsection{Summary of the arithmetic phenomenon}

\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{@{}rlll@{}}
\toprule
Rule & Class & Stationarity relation & Number of stationary configurations \\
\midrule
$30$  & 3 & period two, $s_i = 1 \Rightarrow s_{i-1}=0$ & $3$ if $n$ even, $1$ if $n$ odd \\
$45$  & 3 & period three                                & $3$ if $3 \mid n$, $0$ otherwise \\
$90$  & 3 & $s_{i+1} = s_{i-1}+s_i$, period three       & $4$ if $3 \mid n$, $1$ otherwise \\
$110$ & 4 & $r(1+c+lc) = 0$                             & $1$ for all $n$ \\
$150$ & 3 & $s_{i-1} = s_{i+1}$, period two             & $4$ if $n$ even, $2$ if $n$ odd \\
$204$ & 2 & no constraint                               & $2^n$ \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{center}

A Wolfram class is a single number attached to the rule; these are sequences in
$n$.  The two cannot agree.

\section{Symmetry covariance and the Class 4 orbit}

The $256$ rules carry an action of the Klein four-group generated by
\emph{reflection} $R$ (exchange $l$ and $r$) and \emph{colour inversion} $C$
(complement inputs and output); Wolfram's classification is constant on orbits.
Write $\refl(s)_i = s_{-i}$ and $\conj(s)_i = 1 + s_i$.

\begin{theorem}[Reflection covariance]\label{thm:refl}
If $f_{\rho'}(l,c,r) = f_\rho(r,c,l)$ for all $l,c,r$, then
$\refl(s) \in V(\rho',n) \iff s \in V(\rho,n)$; equivalently
$V(\rho',n) = \refl\bigl(V(\rho,n)\bigr)$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
The $\rho'$-constraint for $\refl(s)$ at cell $i$ involves
$(s_{-i+1}, s_{-i}, s_{-i-1})$ and equals the $\rho$-constraint for $s$ at cell
$-i$; as $i$ ranges over all cells so does $-i$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Conjugation covariance]\label{thm:conj}
If $f_{\rho'}(l,c,r) = 1 + f_\rho(1+l,1+c,1+r)$ for all $l,c,r$, then
$\conj(s) \in V(\rho',n) \iff s \in V(\rho,n)$; equivalently
$V(\rho',n) = \conj\bigl(V(\rho,n)\bigr)$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Substituting $\conj(s)$ into the $\rho'$-constraint and using $1+(1+x) = x$ turns
it into $1 + f_\rho(s_{i-1},s_i,s_{i+1}) = 1 + s_i$, which by cancellation is the
$\rho$-constraint.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[The Class 4 orbit]\label{thm:orbit}
Rule~124 is the reflection of Rule~110, Rule~137 its colour conjugate, and
Rule~193 the reflection of Rule~137.  Consequently, for every $n$,
\[
  V(124,n) = \{0\}, \qquad V(137,n) = \{1\}, \qquad V(193,n) = \{1\},
\]
so every rule in the Turing-complete orbit $\{110,124,137,193\}$ has a one-point
fixed-point variety.  None attains maximal dimension for $n \ge 3$, and the two
odd members $137,193$ have no dimension at all, their single point being the
all-ones configuration rather than the origin.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
The three identities among local rules are finite checks on $\FF_2^3$.  Apply
Theorems~\ref{thm:refl} and~\ref{thm:conj} to Theorem~\ref{thm:110}, noting
$\refl(0) = 0$, $\conj(0) = 1$, $\refl(1) = 1$.  Maximality fails by
Theorem~\ref{thm:max}; the dimension fails to exist for $137,193$ by
Theorem~\ref{thm:odd}.
\end{proof}

\section{A working replacement: the tower of temporal varieties}

\begin{definition}
For $k \ge 0$ set $\Per_k(\rho,n) = \{ s \in \Cfg(n) : F_\rho^{\,k}(s) = s\}$, the
fixed locus of the $k$-fold composite --- a polynomial map of degree at most
$3^k$.  Note $\Per_1(\rho,n) = V(\rho,n)$.
\end{definition}

\begin{lemma}[Return times are closed under gcd]\label{lem:gcd}
Let $g : X \to X$ be any self-map of any set and $x \in X$.  If $g^{k}(x) = x$ and
$g^{\ell}(x) = x$ then $g^{\gcd(k,\ell)}(x) = x$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
First, $g^k(x) = x$ implies $g^{km}(x) = x$ for all $m \ge 0$ by induction.  Now
argue by strong induction on $k$.  If $k = 0$ then $\gcd(0,\ell) = \ell$.  If
$k > 0$, write $\ell = k\lfloor \ell/k\rfloor + (\ell \bmod k)$; then
$x = g^{\ell}(x) = g^{\ell \bmod k}\bigl(g^{k\lfloor \ell/k\rfloor}(x)\bigr)
   = g^{\ell \bmod k}(x)$,
so $\ell \bmod k$ is a return time.  Since $\ell \bmod k < k$, the inductive
hypothesis applied to $(\ell \bmod k, k)$ yields
$g^{\gcd(\ell \bmod k,\,k)}(x) = x$, and $\gcd(\ell \bmod k, k) = \gcd(k,\ell)$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[The tower is a divisibility lattice]\label{thm:lattice}
For all $k,\ell \ge 0$,
\[
  \Per_k(\rho,n) \cap \Per_\ell(\rho,n) = \Per_{\gcd(k,\ell)}(\rho,n),
\]
and $k \mid \ell$ implies $\Per_k(\rho,n) \subseteq \Per_\ell(\rho,n)$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Monotonicity is the first step of the proof of Lemma~\ref{lem:gcd}; the inclusion
$\subseteq$ is Lemma~\ref{lem:gcd}, and $\supseteq$ follows from monotonicity
applied to $\gcd(k,\ell) \mid k$ and $\gcd(k,\ell)\mid \ell$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Bijectivity on each level]\label{thm:bij}
For $k \ge 1$ the automaton restricts to a bijection of $\Per_k(\rho,n)$ onto
itself, with inverse $F_\rho^{\,k-1}$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Write $k = m+1$.  If $F^{m+1}(s) = s$ then $F^{m+1}(F(s)) = F(F^{m+1}(s)) = F(s)$,
so the level is preserved.  If $F(a) = F(b)$ with $a,b$ in the level, applying
$F^m$ gives $a = F^{m+1}(a) = F^{m+1}(b) = b$.  Given $s$ in the level, $F^m(s)$
lies in the level and maps to $F^{m+1}(s) = s$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[The tower of Rule 0 collapses]
For every $k \ge 1$, $\Per_k(0,n) = \{0\}$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
$F_0$ is the constant map to $0$, so $F_0^k(s) = 0$ for $k \ge 1$; equating to $s$
gives $s = 0$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[The tower separates Rule 110 from Rule 0]\label{thm:sep}
On the ring of size $4$ the configuration $1110$ satisfies
$F_{110}(1110) = 1011$ and $F_{110}(1011) = 1110$: a genuine $2$-cycle.  Hence
\[
  \Per_1(110,4) = \Per_1(0,4) = \{0\}
  \quad\text{but}\quad
  \Per_2(110,4) \ne \Per_2(0,4),
\]
and $V(110,4) \subsetneq \Per_2(110,4)$ strictly.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proposition}[Even the repaired invariant is not a dimension]
$\lvert \Per_2(110,4)\rvert = 5$ --- the origin together with the four rotations
forming one $2$-cycle --- and $5 \nmid 16$, so $\Per_2(110,4)$ is not an affine
subvariety of $\AAA^4$.
\end{proposition}

The invariant to pursue is therefore a counting function, not a dimension: the
sequence $k \mapsto \lvert \Per_k(\rho,n)\rvert$, equivalently the dynamical zeta
function
\[
  \zeta_{\rho,n}(t)
   = \exp\left(\sum_{k \ge 1} \frac{\lvert \Per_k(\rho,n)\rvert}{k}\,t^k\right),
\]
whose exponential growth rate is the topological entropy of the automaton on the
ring.

\section{Algorithms and computational structure}

\subsection{The de Bruijn transfer matrix}

Encode a configuration by the sequence of overlapping pairs $(s_{i-1},s_i)$;
there are four such states.  Define the \emph{stationary de Bruijn matrix}
$T_\rho \in \{0,1\}^{4\times 4}$ by
\[
  \bigl(T_\rho\bigr)_{(a,b),\,(b',c)}
   = \begin{cases}
       1 & \text{if } b = b' \text{ and } f_\rho(a,b,c) = b,\\
       0 & \text{otherwise.}
     \end{cases}
\]
A stationary configuration on the ring of $n$ cells is exactly a closed walk of
length $n$ in this graph.

\begin{conjecture}[Transfer-matrix trace formula]\label{conj:trace}
For every rule $\rho$ and every $n \ge 1$,
$\lvert V(\rho,n)\rvert = \tr\bigl(T_\rho^{\,n}\bigr)$.
\end{conjecture}

We have verified this exhaustively for all $256$ rules and all $1 \le n \le 12$.
Granting it, the point counts of every fixed-point variety in the family satisfy
an integer linear recurrence of order at most four --- the characteristic
polynomial of $T_\rho$ --- and are computable in $O(\log n)$ arithmetic
operations.  This explains the arithmetic phenomena of the previous section in a
single stroke, and it is the deepest reason why the Dimension Conjecture cannot
be repaired: four algebraic numbers cannot encode the undecidable question of
whether a rule is computationally universal.

\subsection{Algorithmic summary}

\begin{description}
\item[Algorithm A (Algebraic normal form).] Input $\rho < 256$; output the eight
  coefficients $a_S = \bigoplus_{T \subseteq S} f_\rho(T)$, computed by the
  M\"obius transform on the Boolean lattice with three atoms.  Cost $O(1)$;
  twelve XORs with the in-place butterfly.
\item[Algorithm B (Exhaustive enumeration).] Input $\rho,n$; output $V(\rho,n)$
  by testing the $n$ constraints on all $2^n$ configurations.  Cost $O(n2^n)$.
\item[Algorithm C (Transfer-matrix counting).] Input $\rho,n$; output
  $\lvert V(\rho,n)\rvert$: build $T_\rho$, compute $T_\rho^{\,n}$ by binary
  exponentiation, return the trace.  Cost $O(\log n)$ products of $4\times4$
  matrices, feasible for astronomically large $n$.
\item[Algorithm D (Dimension test).] Input a finite $S \subseteq \FF_2^n$; screen
  $\lvert S\rvert \mid 2^n$, then translate by any $v \in S$ and test that the
  result contains $0$ and is closed under XOR.  Cost $O(\lvert S\rvert^2 n)$, or
  $O(\lvert S\rvert n^2)$ by Gaussian elimination.
\item[Algorithm E (Temporal tower).] Input $\rho,n,K$; build the functional graph
  of $F_\rho$, extract its cycles, and set
  $\lvert\Per_k\rvert = \sum_{d \mid k} d \cdot (\#\text{cycles of length } d)$.
  Cost $O(n2^n + K\log K)$.
\end{description}

\subsection{Selected computational data}

\begin{itemize}
\item $\lvert V(110,n)\rvert = 1$ for all $1 \le n \le 14$, with
      $V(110,n) = V(0,n)$ throughout.
\item The unique rule with $V(\rho,n) = \AAA^n$ is $204$, for each
      $n \in \{3,4,5,6\}$.
\item Exactly $128$ rules omit the origin on the ring of size $5$, and these are
      exactly the odd Wolfram numbers.
\item On the ring of size $6$, only $91$ of the $256$ rules have a linear
      fixed-point variety.
\item $\lvert V(30,n)\rvert = 3$ for even $n \le 14$ and $1$ for odd $n \le 14$,
      the three points always being $\{0,\alpha,\bar\alpha\}$.
\item $\lvert V(90,n)\rvert = 4$ iff $3 \mid n$, else $1$;
      $\lvert V(45,n)\rvert = 3$ iff $3 \mid n$, else $0$;
      $\lvert V(150,n)\rvert = 4$ for even $n$, else $2$ --- all for $n \le 14$.
\item Among $29$ commonly cited representative rules on the ring of size $6$,
      only $3$ satisfy the Dimension Conjecture's prediction; $15$ violate it
      outright and $11$ have no dimension at all.  Every Class~4 rule fails.
\end{itemize}

\section{Discussion}

\subsection{Why the conjecture had to fail}

Three independent explanations converge.  \emph{Structurally}, by
Proposition~\ref{prop:shift} the fixed-point variety is a cyclic subshift of
finite type on two letters with memory two --- the closed walks in a graph on
four vertices --- and the point counts of such objects are traces of powers of
$4\times4$ zero-one matrices.  \emph{Temporally}, the fixed-point variety is the
level $k=1$ of the tower $\Per_k$, precisely the level that discards all
dynamical information; Rules $0$ and $110$ differ in their orbits, and the
difference appears already at $k = 2$.  \emph{Arithmetically}, the natural
parameter controlling $V(\rho,n)$ is $n$ modulo the order of a root of unity in a
small extension of $\FF_2$, whereas Wolfram's class is a function of the rule
alone.

\subsection{Positive residue}

The refutation leaves behind exact, ring-size-uniform determinations of
independent interest:
$V(110,n) = V(124,n) = \{0\}$ and $V(137,n) = V(193,n) = \{1\}$ for all $n$,
including the bi-infinite line; $\dim V(\rho,n) = n$ iff $\rho = 204$ for
$n \ge 3$; $V(30,n) = \{0,\alpha,\bar\alpha\}$ for even $n$ and $\{0\}$ for odd
$n$; $V(90,n) = \{0\}$ exactly when $3 \nmid n$, with $\dim \le 2$ always;
$V(45,n) \ne \varnothing$ exactly when $3 \mid n$; $V(150,n) = \{0,1\}$ for odd
$n$; the Klein-group covariance of $\rho \mapsto V(\rho,n)$; and the lattice
structure $\Per_k \cap \Per_\ell = \Per_{\gcd(k,\ell)}$, valid for an arbitrary
self-map of an arbitrary set, together with bijectivity of the automaton on each
level.

\subsection{Open problems}

\begin{problem}[Trace formula]
Prove Conjecture~\ref{conj:trace} in general and identify the characteristic
polynomial of $T_\rho$ for each rule.  Classify the $256$ rules by the eventual
periodicity type of $n \mapsto \lvert V(\rho,n)\rvert$.
\end{problem}

\begin{problem}[Zeta functions]
Compute $\zeta_{\rho,n}(t)$ for the additive rules, where the update is a
circulant matrix over $\FF_2$ and the periodic-point counts should be expressible
through the factorisation of $x^n - 1$.
\end{problem}

\begin{problem}[Entropy versus class]
Determine whether the growth rate of $\lvert\Per_k(\rho,n)\rvert$ in $k$ --- the
topological entropy --- separates Class~3 from Class~4, and more generally
whether \emph{any} computable invariant of the tower recovers universality.
\end{problem}

\begin{problem}[Scheme structure]
We have worked with $\FF_2$-points.  The scheme
$\operatorname{Spec} \FF_2[x_0,\dots,x_{n-1}] /
 \bigl(f(x_{i-1},x_i,x_{i+1}) - x_i\bigr)$
carries nilpotents; determine whether the length of the structure sheaf at the
origin --- invisible to point counts --- distinguishes Rule~110 from Rule~0.
Theorem~\ref{thm:blind} does not preclude this, and it is the one remaining
loophole in the algebraic-geometric programme.
\end{problem}

\begin{problem}[Higher-radius automata]
For radius-$r$ automata the transfer matrix has size $2^{2r}$.  Determine whether
the fixed-point subshift becomes rich enough to encode universality as
$r \to \infty$, i.e.\ whether the obstruction found here is special to radius one.
\end{problem}

\subsection{Conclusion}

Elementary cellular automata genuinely are algebraic varieties: the $256$ rules
are the $256$ multilinear cubics over $\FF_2$, and their stationary
configurations are the $\FF_2$-points of a cyclic scheme cut out by $n$ cubic
equations.  What does not survive is the hope that this geometry sees complexity.
The fixed-point variety of the Turing-complete Rule~110 is a single point,
identical to that of the rule that erases everything; maximal dimension singles
out the automaton that does nothing; and for most rules there is no dimension to
speak of.  The invariant measures the arithmetic of the ring size, through a
$4\times4$ integer matrix whose spectrum is far too small a container for
universality.  Complexity, if it is algebraic at all, lives in the tower of
temporal varieties --- in the zeta function, not in a dimension.

